 | | |
 | --- | --- |
-| Default | 2.0 |
-| Range | clamped to 0.25-4.0 in use |
-| Purpose | Exaggeration exponent for the polygonal bokeh kernel. 1 = physical radius modulation (very subtle at high blade counts: 19% for a pentagon, 8% for an octagon, nearly invisible after the quarter-resolution gather and TAA); 2-3 = clearly readable polygons; 4 = star-like caricature. |
+| Default | 1.0 |
+| Range | input clamped to 0.25-4.0, then the resulting exponent is clamped to 0-1 |
+| Purpose | Formation exponent for the polygonal bokeh kernel, applied to both the gather taps and the splat's polygon. 1 = physical: the bokeh is exactly the iris polygon. Below 1 the polygon is rounded back toward a circle (0 = a perfect disc). Above 1 has no effect - it is clamped. |
+
+A real iris opening is the intersection of its blades and is therefore always **convex**, so
+nothing may push its corners past the true polygon. The old 2-4 range did exactly that: the
+radius function is normalised to the edge midpoints, so a corner sits 8% out for an octagon but
+**2.0x** out for a triangle, and an exponent of 2 gave that triangle 4x-radius spikes - a
+concave cloverleaf instead of an aperture.
 
 Not overwritten by the plugin; tune it from the console at any time. Blade Curvature feeds the
-same exponent: straight blades (curvature 0) run it at double strength and stay fully formed at
-every aperture.
+same exponent: straight blades (curvature 0) stay fully formed at every aperture instead of
+following the f/1.2 - f/2.8 window. Curvature's other half is the polygon's **edge softness**
+in the splat (0.06 pupil-radius units when fully curved, 0.015 when fully straight), which is
+what makes a straight blade read as sharper without deforming the silhouette.
 
